          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "If a clinician writes diabetes in one place and cataract in another, does the \"with\" convention connect them?",
          "answer": "In medical terminology, the \"with\" convention describes how two conditions can be linked in coding and documentation. When the alphabetic index connects two conditions using the word \"with,\" it is interpreted as \"associated with\" or \"due to.\" This means that if one condition is noted alongside another, it assumes a relationship without needing explicit documentation stating the two are directly connected. For example, if a record mentions diabetes and chronic kidney disease, it is coded as diabetic kidney disease unless stated otherwise. This is outlined in documents from the US clinical modification of ICD-10 (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11 and coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions).\n\nRegarding diabetes and cataracts, there is a known association. Diabetes can lead to complications including ocular problems, such as cataracts. When diabetes is mentioned and cataracts are noted separately, the context may imply that the cataracts are a complication associated with the diabetes, following the \"with\" convention in documentation. This relationship is supported in various guidelines pertaining to diabetes care, indicating that managing diabetes effectively can help reduce the risk of such complications (guideline-type-2-diabetes-glycemic-targets). \n\nIn summary, if diabetes and cataracts are documented in the same record, the \"with\" convention can connect them, suggesting a relationship where the cataracts might be related to the diabetes without needing more explicit language.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
